{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"code-analysis-skills","displayName":"Code Analysis Skills","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats/skills/code-analysis-skills","publisherHandle":"wscats","publisherDisplayName":"enoyao","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats","version":"1.1.1","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1780377614153,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/code-analysis-skills/card?ownerHandle=wscats&version=1.1.1","sha256":"01387c55b8bcc2ac558cf64e6c5ab2d22bd067475df8c3512229d7333b5976ac","size":2393,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"4b4835f41d8bc8db2086829a5f47f46032398547726d0e14ef8740ad5b407a71","bundleFingerprints":["ab3c964b45b0fee495de8523bc9b16b25046f1991e040bfa328bad3fcb84606f","6970c78d8f9ea88fc0bf897eb028cf98d06fde7125386d5572a852d3029e11d1","ee24cfc1ee6487e7909861d2c773f48ce6dd6afc50f9a3d98a0cd6d1c5aaffbe"],"files":[{"path":"README.CN.md","size":11495,"sha256":"658e6ce37ba6c6834e45db32b13ae831dc860cdb7fa42a9934d44780633aee0b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"README.EN.md","size":13049,"sha256":"64f9e917ceee8ed9138b01ef81b33fb7b277fb8a40f42effce3d6180e596aabd","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"README.md","size":10021,"sha256":"0362218a3abbf1ebdf10d147ee43a1148f46ff9d9a03819964d1cff882b642f0","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.cn.md","size":15757,"sha256":"fe582d0293731484bcf56a62f8b0c0a15db8f48e711820a24798e5821be1a3f6","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":17759,"sha256":"b4ec534d9bbf91177d00db7889d096191cab53fe57bc6bfe28f202eec0617e30","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"publish.sh","size":3719,"sha256":"0d2ead2b9ef4a48e7dee1d5daaeb97624809d04d7ab08c6473eeb07b5798f118","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"pyproject.toml","size":722,"sha256":"88ef60d94808f31003bc86ec77933147c24f3b704d10542f0282326094288fcb","contentType":"application/toml"},{"path":"pytest.ini","size":125,"sha256":"9024eb52b07a0ae4471be8219bdb57948721e4932387e9bb152d8fc52af28952","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"references/metrics-guide.md","size":8143,"sha256":"fb6ef6cc564484d44d1461e3ea0b75b9bda53c2086e25345475237b1d70ac0b8","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"requirements.txt","size":118,"sha256":"155a16d803560aa07e9d3b9a4f8e12814da1b1ac99fb7511d9fbd4a39a918cca","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill.yaml","size":5749,"sha256":"4656963991543fe39d4e5e8667afd06a5a97c692e3ce60e3df521ca857dd0a97","contentType":"text/yaml"},{"path":"src/__init__.py","size":23,"sha256":"13890eb0a6a1a991d467d87d3fa29e46a1a39ac717b39c0faccd1117b2e34944","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/__init__.py","size":19,"sha256":"069c9675177ab2a41697d6bd4e6ebb2d327685e8e2f70750abdc7e0cc9f6fb0e","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/base_analyzer.py","size":4142,"sha256":"7c30fe7e3c422bc7dcb8393cd63af44b34caaeeee1719a1f00f0def5af217de8","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/cadence_signal_analyzer.py","size":10872,"sha256":"c89e952e224784764f6329c1c54b448364e210a5fb9f0ce6fd237d24cd427d3b","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/code_quality_analyzer.py","size":6969,"sha256":"e7d2adb32fcc276ff778e3f507b396c66109ece000493af877e0d859ce806f39","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/code_style_analyzer.py","size":5748,"sha256":"e8066fb2c2c71c8d64af114971e97675d73507d9e91e0c63be63356f41d2b1ef","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/commit_analyzer.py","size":4113,"sha256":"df6be02a3b5a44bbbcaad43eef96c6c470d59e225a145ce6b7b8a93699767bf1","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/efficiency_analyzer.py","size":7871,"sha256":"276976a0f453d26b001459c5b79bc6edc720871acedc88282dfc0ac5c7826a6d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/analyzers/work_habit_analyzer.py","size":7844,"sha256":"f5a4818c97d6c798f7c1c96adb579ef6066b823eb47e3e6223df1911f5442809","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/main.py","size":20113,"sha256":"5ad3270ed425ef4368d5a1399e6e8734cd44fa5ad7458d94f93be7d3fd261fc8","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/narrator/__init__.py","size":20,"sha256":"f4ec6585cf16815594ede5a0fc3fb0a1958188ff6a8b19a991cff48b8fd9cdc8","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/narrator/reflection_narrator.py","size":17640,"sha256":"0b829757ef9c92e6671845bfc6f27570d1948195832709659eef003f2bb5ac78","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/__init__.py","size":19,"sha256":"ad219ca24982c438dca5878af853a9e83cc8aec649a2f71f4b3107a4c198aa0f","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/base_reporter.py","size":522,"sha256":"e0cc9421eab8735f1f1d4c106e1f1c99423c6b5166e59b81873752ab83860959","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/html_reporter.py","size":15563,"sha256":"46b40b8f813ca36cbdc685ce91c69027b08d3635fcdae61e97976d7aba510178","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/json_reporter.py","size":967,"sha256":"f97e59d1f472a054314dc235e9e1d32f8da8500ed93b5f48b1cd7fdafc99cd10","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/markdown_reporter.py","size":13172,"sha256":"5e7bba108dff56c1116386b6083328afc38f625d850037fb8bfd6d56c8071390","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/reporters/pdf_reporter.py","size":17608,"sha256":"90b485bcc262daa99511cf1bce829bf37c305ae45cd517fa6398010cd132be2e","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/scanner.py","size":5820,"sha256":"38b705b65834d0c305af923952183bfb9e6de5debf8f0e968a0316f3caf91b4f","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/utils/__init__.py","size":15,"sha256":"d239548b903d4254216b62409b94c72e14cfe25cbb31165be200be5889099ef4","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"src/utils/helpers.py","size":1379,"sha256":"f405727d82af3b049ede515177928b30ca8f4a3f37b1f9dad30aeb68252bb55f","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"tests/__init__.py","size":16,"sha256":"5a4ef7228eb6275e41b652d5233c660120d669a26440b7af4b804b2b92c30108","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"tests/test_analyzers.py","size":3819,"sha256":"c939f2e686db14623bf8ff9cff968985bd05966c6d52e0a78dbcfc33f0f8a324","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"tests/test_scanner.py","size":2598,"sha256":"deae14f6f8a92f797e4a9273e14d08c2d08b28f9f5561a4aa4c9ac710473e417","contentType":"text/plain"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill is a disclosed local Git-history reflection tool, but its handling of personal developer data has consent-scoping and report-rendering risks that need review.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789170788932,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T05","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Substring-Based Author Matching Can Analyze Non-Consenting Identities"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"src/analyzers/base_analyzer.py"},"region":{"startLine":82,"endLine":96}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"8b4d27dbc4f1e0bd"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `src/analyzers/base_analyzer.py:82-96`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Inadequate identity authorization and ambiguous substring matching  \n**Risk Level**: Medium  \n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```python\ntry:\n    for commit in Repository(**kwargs).traverse_commits():\n        if self.authors:\n            if not self._author_matches(commit.author.name, commit.author.email):\n                continue\n        yield commit\nexcept Exception as e:\n    logger.error(\"Error traversing commits in %s: %s\", self.repo_path, e)\n\ndef _author_matches(self, name: str, email: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Check whether a commit's author name/email is in the\n    consent-resolved identity set populated by the orchestrator.\"\"\"\n    for author_filter in self.authors:\n        af_lower = author_filter.lower()\n        if af_lower in name.lower() or af_lower in email.lower():\n            return True\n    return False\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe consent enforcement mechanism uses unrestricted, case-insensitive substring matching instead of matching complete canonical identities. A consent filter such as `Ann` will match not only an author named `Ann`, but also identities such as:\n\n- `Joanne`\n- `Annette`\n- `ann-attacker@example.com`\n- `not-ann@example.org`\n\nThe same issue affects self-scope mode because the local Git user's name and email are passed into this matching function. Although a complete email generally reduces ambiguity, the local name is also included as an independent filter, and a matching result from either filter is sufficient.\n\nThis behavior conflicts with the stated guarantee that only the current user or explicitly listed consenting authors are analyzed. Git author metadata is not authenticated and can be freely selected when creating a commit, so it must not be treated as a reliable authorization identifier through fuzzy matching.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. A legitimate developer named `Ann` consents to analysis, or the local Git configuration identifies the operator as `Ann`.\n2. Another contributor creates commits using an identity such as `Ann-Contractor` or `ann-attacker@example.com`.\n3. The operator runs the Skill with `Ann` as a consented author or in self-scope mode.\n4. `_author_matches()` finds `ann` inside the unintended contributor's name or email.\n5. The unintended contributor's commits pass the authorization filter.\n6. All analyzers process those commits and include their timestamps, change statistics, cadence indicators, and other Git-history data in the resulting metrics and reports.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe flaw does not grant operating-system privileges or repository write access. Its impact is an authorization-boundary failure over personal Git activity data.\n\nAn unintended or non-consenting contributor may have the following information processed and disclosed:\n\n- Commit timestamps and activity cadence\n- Weekend and late-night commit ratios\n- Commit frequency and message statistics\n- Changed files and code-change volume\n- Churn, rework, and ownership indicators\n- Code-style and code-quality artifacts\n\nThe scope is limited to repositories selected for analysis, but `--scan-all` can cause the issue to affect multiple repositories beneath the selected directory.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Replace substring matching with exact matching against normalized identities.\n2. Prefer complete normalized email addresses as identifiers:\n   - Strip surrounding whitespace.\n   - Convert the domain portion to lowercase.\n   - Compare the entire address rather than using `in`.\n3. If names must be supported, resolve each requested name against repository identities before analysis.\n4. Reject ambiguous names that correspond to more than one name/email pair.\n5. Represent authorized identities as explicit pairs, for example:\n\n```python\ndef _author_matches(self, name: str, email: str) -> bool:\n    normalized_name = (name or \"\").strip().casefold()\n    normalized_email = (email or \"\").strip().casefold()\n\n    for identity in self.authorized_identities:\n        if normalized_email and normalized_email == identity.email:\n            return True\n        if (\n            identity.email is None\n            and normalized_name\n            and normalized_name == identity.name\n        ):\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```\n\n6. In self-scope mode, require an exact email match when a local Git email is configured. Do not also accept arbitrary names containing the local user's name.\n7. Add tests for colliding identities such as `Ann`, `Joanne`, `Annette`, and `ann-attacker@example.com`.\n8. Record the exact resolved identities in scope metadata so the report accurately describes whose commits were processed.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Unescaped Git and Filesystem Metadata Enables Stored HTML Injection"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"src/reporters/html_reporter.py"},"region":{"startLine":159,"endLine":346}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"7dc4805cb256690b"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `src/reporters/html_reporter.py:159-179, 343-346`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Stored HTML injection caused by disabled template autoescaping  \n**Risk Level**: Medium  \n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```html\n{% for repo_name, repo_metrics in metrics.items() %}\n<h2>📁 {{ repo_name }}</h2>\n\n{% set scope = repo_metrics.get('_scope', {}) %}\n{% if scope.get('mode') == 'self_scope' %}\n<div class=\"scope-banner\">\n    🔒 <strong>Scope: self-reflection only.</strong> Analysis is locked to the\n    local Git user (<code>{{ scope.filters | join(' / ') }}</code>). Other authors\n    in the repository are not analysed.\n</div>\n{% elif scope.get('mode') == 'team_retro' %}\n<div class=\"scope-banner\">\n    👥 <strong>Scope: consented team retrospective.</strong> Caller has explicitly\n    asserted informed consent from every listed author:\n    {% for a in scope.filters %}<code>{{ a }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.\n</div>\n```\n\n```html\n{% for author in all_authors | sort %}\n<div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>👤 {{ author }}</h3>\n```\n\n```python\nclass HtmlReporter(BaseReporter):\n    \"\"\"Generates styled HTML reports from analysis metrics.\"\"\"\n\n    def generate(self, metrics: Dict) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Generate an HTML report using Jinja2 template.\"\"\"\n        template = Template(HTML_TEMPLATE)\n        return template.render(metrics=metrics)\n```\n\nRelevant attacker-controlled data sources include:\n\n```python\nreturn {\n    \"name\": os.path.basename(path),\n    \"path\": path,\n    \"active_branch\": active_branch,\n    \"remotes\": [r.name for r in repo.remotes] if repo.remotes else [],\n}\n```\n\nfrom `src/scanner.py:151-156`, and:\n\n```python\nauthor = commit.author.name\n```\n\nfrom analyzer implementations such as `src/analyzers/commit_analyzer.py:56`.\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe reporter constructs the template directly with `jinja2.Template`. This does not enable automatic HTML escaping. Values inserted with `{{ ... }}` are therefore rendered as raw markup.\n\nThe affected values include:\n\n- Repository directory names\n- Git author names\n- Local Git identity values\n- Explicit `consented_authors` parameter values\n- Other strings propagated through report metrics\n\nGit author names are attacker-controlled metadata. A contributor can create a commit under a name containing HTML, such as an image element with an error handler. A repository directory name or supplied consent filter can similarly contain markup.\n\nWhen the generated report is opened in a browser, the injected value is interpreted as HTML rather than displayed as text. Depending on browser security controls and the injected payload, this can modify the report, execute JavaScript, create deceptive links, or initiate external resource requests.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An attacker creates a commit with an author name containing an HTML payload.\n2. The commit is introduced into a repository that the victim later analyzes.\n3. The attacker-controlled author metadata becomes a key in analyzer output.\n4. `HtmlReporter` inserts that author key into the template through `{{ author }}`.\n5. Because autoescaping is disabled, the payload is written verbatim into the HTML report.\n6. The victim opens the generated report in a web browser.\n7. The browser interprets the injected markup in the local report's security context.\n\nA comparable path exists when an attacker can influence the repository directory name or a caller supplies markup in `consented_authors`.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe issue does not directly execute code during ordinary Markdown or JSON generation. It affects generated HTML and the HTML content subsequently used by PDF conversion.\n\nPotential effects include:\n\n- Arbitrary modification of the visible report\n- Insertion of fraudulent findings, warnings, or links\n- Script execution where permitted by the browser\n- External requests that disclose that the report was opened\n- Exposure of information accessible to scripts in the report's origin, subject to browser restrictions\n\nThe exploit runs with the privileges and browser context of the person opening the report. Modern browser restrictions may limit access from a `file://` document, but they do not make raw HTML injection safe.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Create a Jinja environment with HTML autoescaping enabled:\n\n```python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, BaseLoader, select_autoescape\n\nclass HtmlReporter(BaseReporter):\n    def generate(self, metrics: Dict) -> str:\n        environment = Environment(\n            loader=BaseLoader(),\n            autoescape=select_autoescape(\n                enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"xml\"),\n                default_for_string=True,\n                default=True,\n            ),\n        )\n        template = environment.from_string(HTML_TEMPLATE)\n        return template.render(metrics=metrics)\n```\n\n2. Apply `|e` explicitly to security-sensitive values as defense in depth:\n\n```html\n<h2>📁 {{ repo_name | e }}</h2>\n<h3>👤 {{ author | e }}</h3>\n<code>{{ a | e }}</code>\n```\n\n3. Avoid applying `join` before escaping individual elements. Escape each filter value independently inside a loop.\n4. Treat every value originating from Git metadata, paths, CLI parameters, or Skill parameters as untrusted.\n5. Add a restrictive Content Security Policy to generated reports, for example one that blocks scripts and remote resources.\n6. Add regression tests using:\n   - `<script>` elements\n   - Event-handler attributes\n   - `<img src=x onerror=...>`\n   - Quotes and malformed tags\n   - HTML-bearing author and repository names\n7. Verify that rendered reports contain escaped entities such as `&lt;` and `&gt;`, not executable tags.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"PDF Conversion Processes Untrusted HTML Without a Resource-Access Policy"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"src/reporters/pdf_reporter.py"},"region":{"startLine":49,"endLine":91}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"1b387d06c1c4cede"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `src/reporters/pdf_reporter.py:49-91`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unsafe processing of attacker-influenced HTML by PDF conversion engines  \n**Risk Level**: Medium  \n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```python\ndef generate_to_file(self, metrics: Dict, output_path: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generate a PDF report and write it to a file.\n\n    Args:\n        metrics: Analysis metrics dict.\n        output_path: Path to write the PDF file.\n\n    Returns:\n        The output file path.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Try HTML-to-PDF engines first for best visual quality\n    html_content = self.generate(metrics)\n    for method in [\n        self._try_weasyprint,\n        self._try_pdfkit,\n        self._try_xhtml2pdf,\n    ]:\n        try:\n            return method(html_content, output_path)\n        except (ImportError, Exception) as e:\n            logger.debug(\"PDF method failed: %s\", e)\n            continue\n\n    # Fall back to reportlab (always available)\n    logger.info(\"Using reportlab for PDF generation\")\n    return self._generate_with_reportlab(metrics, output_path)\n```\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _try_weasyprint(html_content: str, output_path: str) -> str:\n    from weasyprint import HTML\n    HTML(string=html_content).write_pdf(output_path)\n    logger.info(\"PDF generated with weasyprint: %s\", output_path)\n    return output_path\n\n@staticmethod\ndef _try_pdfkit(html_content: str, output_path: str) -> str:\n    import pdfkit\n    pdfkit.from_string(html_content, output_path, options={\n        \"encoding\": \"UTF-8\", \"page-size\": \"A4\",\n        \"margin-top\": \"15mm\", \"margin-bottom\": \"15mm\",\n    })\n    logger.info(\"PDF generated with pdfkit: %s\", output_path)\n    return output_path\n\n@staticmethod\ndef _try_xhtml2pdf(html_content: str, output_path: str) -> str:\n    from xhtml2pdf import pisa\n    with open(output_path, \"wb\") as f:\n        pisa.CreatePDF(html_content, dest=f)\n    logger.info(\"PDF generated with xhtml2pdf: %s\", output_path)\n    return output_path\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nPDF generation consumes the HTML produced by `HtmlReporter`, which does not escape attacker-controlled Git and filesystem metadata. The resulting raw markup is passed to WeasyPrint, pdfkit/wkhtmltopdf, or xhtml2pdf.\n\nNo converter-specific security policy is configured to:\n\n- Deny remote HTTP or HTTPS requests\n- Deny access to local files\n- Restrict accepted URL schemes\n- Allow only embedded static resources\n- Reject active or unsupported markup\n\nAn injected image, stylesheet, font, or similar resource reference may therefore cause a converter-dependent request during PDF generation. Exact support varies by the installed engine and version, but the code does not establish a safe, consistent policy.\n\nThe ReportLab fallback also constructs `Paragraph` objects from repository and author strings without escaping. ReportLab parses a markup subset, so attacker-controlled identity values should not be passed directly to `Paragraph`.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An attacker creates Git metadata containing an HTML resource element or markup-bearing identity.\n2. The victim analyzes the repository and requests PDF output.\n3. `HtmlReporter.generate()` preserves the attacker-controlled markup because autoescaping is disabled.\n4. `PdfReporter.generate_to_file()` sends the resulting HTML to the first available conversion engine.\n5. The converter parses the injected markup.\n6. If the installed converter permits the referenced resource type and URL scheme, it attempts to access the attacker-selected resource.\n7. The attacker can observe an outbound request, or the converter may access a local resource depending on its capabilities and configuration.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe impact depends on which optional converter is installed and its default security behavior.\n\nPotential consequences include:\n\n- Outbound requests from the machine generating the report\n- Disclosure of source IP address and request metadata\n- Access to internal HTTP services reachable from the host\n- Attempted local-resource access where supported by the converter\n- Corruption or deceptive modification of the resulting PDF\n- Denial of service through slow, oversized, or repeatedly referenced resources\n\nThis finding does not establish unrestricted local-file exfiltration or code execution. Those outcomes would require additional converter-specific behavior or a separate disclosure channel. The confirmed weakness is that untrusted markup reaches several complex rendering engines without escaping or a resource-access policy.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Correct the HTML escaping issue before passing report content to any PDF engine.\n2. For WeasyPrint, supply a restrictive URL fetcher that rejects all non-embedded resources:\n\n```python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\nfrom weasyprint import HTML, default_url_fetcher\n\ndef restricted_fetcher(url):\n    parsed = urlparse(url)\n    if parsed.scheme not in {\"data\"}:\n        raise ValueError(\"External and local resource access is disabled\")\n    return default_url_fetcher(url)\n\nHTML(\n    string=html_content,\n    url_fetcher=restricted_fetcher,\n).write_pdf(output_path)\n```\n\n3. For pdfkit/wkhtmltopdf:\n   - Disable JavaScript.\n   - Disable local-file access.\n   - Block external links and remote resources where supported.\n   - Use an explicit allowlist if static assets are required.\n4. Consider removing converter fallbacks whose resource-access behavior cannot be consistently hardened.\n5. Prefer embedded CSS and fonts so PDF generation requires no external resources.\n6. Escape ReportLab strings with `xml.sax.saxutils.escape` before passing them to `Paragraph`:\n\n```python\nfrom xml.sax.saxutils import escape\n\nelements.append(\n    Paragraph(f\"Repository: {escape(str(repo_name))}\", h2_style)\n)\nelements.append(\n    Paragraph(f\"Author: {escape(str(author))}\", h3_style)\n)\n```\n\n7. Execute PDF conversion in a sandbox with:\n   - No network access\n   - A read-only filesystem\n   - A dedicated temporary working directory\n   - CPU, memory, file-size, and execution-time limits\n8. Add tests that inject remote URLs, `file://` URLs, markup-bearing author names, oversized resources, and unsupported schemes. Assert that no network or local-file request occurs.\n]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":25,"primaryLanguage":"Python","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789170599.5911753,"endTime":1789170723.7168345}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"code-analysis","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57dyk0201esadjwtt7sd24nt18e7wrq-gWhh1y/clawscan-skillspector-3607755977/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-11T23:49:58.384603+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"README.CN.md","type":"markdown","lines":229,"executable":false,"size_bytes":11495,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"README.EN.md","type":"markdown","lines":238,"executable":false,"size_bytes":13049,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"README.md","type"